---
# AutoKuma static entity files (KD1). Filename (minus .json) = the AutoKuma id, and each id
# here MUST equal the label-derived id it replaces — the seeded entity map is keyed by id,
# and a mismatch makes AutoKuma create a duplicate instead of adopting (KD5 gate finding).
#
# This Secret is the single declaration home for every monitor and both notifications; the
# transcription from the pre-migration label declarations finished with the migration. A
# Secret, not a ConfigMap: push tokens, the Discord webhook and the SMTP password are values,
# not references, once rendered.
#
# Field names are v2.0.0 serde: `maxretries` (http/port/dns/ping), `max_retries` and
# `push_token` accepted as aliases on push, `accepted_statuscodes`, `notification_name_list`.
# Omitted accepted_statuscodes defaults to ["200-299"] server-side, same as the labels did.
#
# REMOVING AN ENTRY HERE MAY NOT REACH THE CLUSTER: this Secret predates apply-managed
# creation, so it carried no last-applied baseline and `kubectl apply` never pruned
# removed keys — the "(via bridge)" retirement (2026-08-10) deployed green while the old
# files stayed mounted and autokuma faithfully re-created every monitor kuma-cli deleted.
# Fixed with a one-time `kubectl replace` (the baseline exists now, so apply prunes
# from here on). After a removal, verify the monitor is gone and kuma-cli-delete it if
# not (pattern: kuma-secret-replace.yml in the job archive).
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: kuma-static-monitors
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
type: Opaque
stringData:
  # ── notifications (label-provisioned before; file-provisioned now — KD5 gate) ──────────
  #
  # `applyExisting: false` looks inert and is load-bearing. Kuma's save() forces the key into
  # the stored config on EVERY write (server/notification.js: `notification.applyExisting =
  # false` before `JSON.stringify`), and AutoKuma's config_eq compares the NUMBER of config keys
  # after dropping six ignored ones — a set that does not include applyExisting. So a config
  # declared without it is permanently one key short of what Kuma stores, never compares equal,
  # and gets rewritten on every sync pass: 2 writes per pass, ~34k SQLite writes a day onto a
  # Longhorn volume whose changed blocks the nightly backup then ships. The monitors converge
  # fine; only these two objects looped, from the k3s cutover until 2026-08-21.
  #
  # The value must be false. Kuma reads the flag before forcing it (`applyExisting ||
  # false`) and a true would attach the notification to every existing monitor.
  #
  # Kuma's `webhook` provider, not its `discord` one, since 2026-09-18. Both POST to the same
  # Discord webhook; the difference is who writes the body. The discord provider's own custom
  # template (`discordMessageFormat: custom`) sends plain `content` and drops the embed, while
  # the webhook provider with `webhookContentType: custom` renders `webhookCustomBody` through
  # the same liquidjs engine and posts the result verbatim — so the body is the embed we
  # author, in files/discord-message.liquid, one template every monitor shares. The header is
  # load-bearing: axios sends a string body as application/x-www-form-urlencoded and Discord
  # rejects that. The AutoKuma id stays `discord` so no monitor's notification_name_list moves,
  # and the name stays "Homelab Alerts" because monitor-bridge's Kuma Notification Delivery
  # check reads it out of Kuma's `Cannot send notification to <name>` line.
  #
  # The Liquid body is wrapped in Tera's raw block. AutoKuma runs EVERY entity through Tera
  # before it parses it (autokuma/src/entity.rs, get_entity_from_settings — unconditional,
  # `files.preprocess` gates only an earlier pass), and Tera reads Liquid's comment tag and
  # its double-brace interpolations as its own syntax (so does this Jinja file, which is why
  # neither is spelled out here): on 2026-09-18 the parse failed, AutoKuma treated the
  # notification as undeclared and re-synced all 108 monitors with an empty notification
  # list. Tera strips the raw markers and passes the content verbatim, so Kuma receives the
  # Liquid unchanged. The markers are assembled from pieces so Jinja does not read them, and
  # the two `set` lines sit at column 0 so trim_blocks leaves no indent behind.
{% set tera_raw = '{%' ~ ' raw ' ~ '%}' %}
{% set tera_endraw = '{%' ~ ' endraw ' ~ '%}' %}
  discord.json: |
    {
      "type": "notification",
      "name": "Homelab Alerts",
      "active": true,
      "config": {"type": "webhook", "applyExisting": false, "webhookURL": "{{ monitor_discord_webhook_url }}", "webhookContentType": "custom", "webhookAdditionalHeaders": "{\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"}", "webhookCustomBody": {{ (tera_raw ~ lookup('file', playbook_dir + '/roles/k8s/uptime-kuma/files/discord-message.liquid') ~ tera_endraw) | to_json }}}
    }
  # Fans out to BOTH channels: the Discord dead-man's-switch below, plus the critical tier
  # (both backup targets, disks, SMART, SD, cert expiry, UPS, and the home-critical services' own
  # tiles) — a *degraded* Discord loses individual alerts while the watchdog stays green, and
  # those are the ones that can't wait a day. "Both backup targets" was "backup/B2" until 2026-08-21: B2's headroom tile was on this
  # tier and R2's was not, though R2 is a Longhorn backup target too and a full bucket stops its
  # backups the same way. The membership test is what a monitor guards, not which vendor it names.
  email.json: |
    {
      "type": "notification",
      "name": "Homelab Alerts (Email)",
      "active": true,
      "config": {"type": "smtp", "applyExisting": false, "smtpHost": "smtp.gmail.com", "smtpPort": 465, "smtpSecure": true, "smtpUsername": "{{ email }}", "smtpPassword": "{{ smtp_notify_app_password }}", "smtpFrom": "{{ email }}", "smtpTo": "{{ email }}", "customSubject": {{ (tera_raw ~ '[Homelab] {{ status }} {{ name }}' ~ tera_endraw) | to_json }}, "customBody": {{ (tera_raw ~ lookup('file', playbook_dir + '/roles/k8s/uptime-kuma/files/email-message.liquid') ~ tera_endraw) | to_json }}}
    }
  # The two tags the notification templates read. A monitor names one in `tag_names`
  # (`[{"name": "tag-severity", "value": "critical"}]` — the tag ENTITY's AutoKuma id, the filename
  # minus .json, not the name Kuma displays) and AutoKuma resolves that id to the tag it created
  # here; the templates print the value when present and nothing when
  # absent. Same NameNotFound hazard as the notification (#2076): a monitor naming a tag
  # this Secret no longer declares fails to parse and is deleted under ON_DELETE=delete.
  # Colours are Catppuccin Mocha red and blue, which is what the Kuma UI shows them in.
  tag-severity.json: |
    {"type": "tag", "name": "severity", "color": "#f38ba8"}
  tag-runbook.json: |
    {"type": "tag", "name": "runbook", "color": "#89b4fa"}
  # ── daniel-pi LAN probes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  daniel-pi-host.json: |
    {"type": "ping", "name": "Daniel Pi Host", "hostname": "{{ hostvars['daniel-pi'].server_ip }}", "interval": 60, "maxretries": 2, "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
  daniel-pi-wg-easy.json: |
    {"type": "http", "name": "Daniel Pi WG Easy", "url": "http://{{ hostvars['daniel-pi'].server_ip }}:51821", "interval": 60, "maxretries": 2, "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
  # A home-critical service's own availability tile pages like the infrastructure tiles do:
  # `severity: critical` plus the email leg, decided by the operator on 2026-10-04 (#3480).
  # The macro reads the entry's `tier` from containers_list, so tiering a service puts its tile
  # on the email tier and untiering one takes it off. The tag and the email list come out
  # together because `test_severity_critical_is_exactly_the_email_tier` holds them as one fact.
  # An argument naming no containers_list entry fails the render, not the page.
{% macro service_alerting(entry) -%}
{% if containers_list | in_service_tier(entry, 'home-critical') -%}
"tag_names": [{"name": "tag-severity", "value": "critical"}], "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}", "email"]
{%- else -%}
"notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]
{%- endif %}
{%- endmacro %}
  # ── k3s ingress monitors ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  auth-k8s.json: |
    {"type": "http", "name": "k3s Authelia Portal", "url": "https://auth.local.{{ domain }}", "interval": 60, "maxretries": 2, {{ service_alerting('authelia') }}}
  bento-pdf-k8s.json: |
    {"type": "http", "name": "k3s Ingress (bento-pdf)", "url": "https://bento-pdf.local.{{ domain }}", "interval": 60, "maxretries": 2, "accepted_statuscodes": ["302"], "max_redirects": 0, "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
  littlelink-k8s.json: |
    {"type": "http", "name": "k3s littlelink", "url": "https://www.local.{{ domain }}", "interval": 60, "maxretries": 2, "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
  speedtest-k8s.json: |
    {"type": "http", "name": "k3s speedtest", "url": "https://speedtest.local.{{ domain }}", "interval": 60, "maxretries": 2, "accepted_statuscodes": ["302"], "max_redirects": 0, "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
  freshrss-k8s.json: |
    {"type": "http", "name": "k3s FreshRSS", "url": "https://freshrss.local.{{ domain }}", "interval": 60, "maxretries": 2, "accepted_statuscodes": ["302"], "max_redirects": 0, "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
  headlamp-k8s.json: |
    {"type": "http", "name": "k3s Headlamp", "url": "https://headlamp.local.{{ domain }}", "interval": 60, "maxretries": 2, "accepted_statuscodes": ["302"], "max_redirects": 0, "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
  # Probes the edge's LAN-only `livesync-probe` router (roles/k8s/traefik/templates/
  # livesync-gate-secret.yaml.j2), the one token-free path on this host: k8s edge -> probe
  # router -> CouchDB. A 401 is CouchDB's require_valid_user answering unauthenticated, the
  # end-to-end signal; any other path without the X-Sync-Token matches no router and 404s.
  # The only LiveSync tile. `livesync-bridge.json` ("LiveSync (edge gate)") probed this same
  # URL after the 2026-08-12 gate move, so one outage paged twice; it was retired (#2782).
  livesync-k8s.json: |
    {"type": "http", "name": "k3s LiveSync", "url": "https://livesync.local.{{ domain }}/", "interval": 60, "maxretries": 2, "accepted_statuscodes": ["401"], "max_redirects": 0, "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
  mosquitto-k8s.json: |
    {"type": "port", "name": "k3s Mosquitto (VIP)", "hostname": "{{ mqtt_k8s_vip }}", "port": 1883, "interval": 60, "maxretries": 2, {{ service_alerting('mosquitto') }}}
  # VIP port tiles (kube-proxy ETP-Local DROP mode): joining a Docker host to k3s can
  # black out a container's VIP while host-side probes stay green, so a real cross-host
  # TCP check like this is what actually catches it.
  jellyfin-lan-vip.json: |
    {"type": "port", "name": "k3s Jellyfin (VIP)", "hostname": "{{ jellyfin_k8s_lan_ip }}", "port": 8096, "interval": 60, "maxretries": 2, "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
  # terraria's port tile was HERE and is deliberately gone (2026-09-02, issue #822). It is the
  # only thing left that opened a TCP connection to the game server, and on Terraria 1.4.5.7 a
  # connect-and-drop kills the server loop: `TcpSocket.IsConnected` now reads
  # `DebugNetworkStream.HasBufferedData` -> `NetworkStream.DataAvailable`, which throws
  # `ObjectDisposedException` on a stream the peer already closed, unhandled, on the server
  # thread. It crashed terraria 8 times in 51 minutes, every crash on this tile's probe tick.
  # 1.4.5.6 had no such property in the binary and took the same probe for weeks without a
  # crash. The workload is scaled to zero for now (terraria_k8s_replicas in group_vars/all.yml),
  # which would make
  # this tile permanently red regardless — but the crash, not the scale-down, is why it must not
  # come back with the server. Restoring it needs an image past 1.4.5.7; #822 carries the check.
  # wg-easy's VIP is WireGuard-over-UDP — Kuma port monitors are TCP-only, so it cannot get
  # a port-check tile here.
  zigbee2mqtt-k8s.json: |
    {"type": "http", "name": "k3s Zigbee2MQTT", "url": "https://zigbee2mqtt.local.{{ domain }}", "interval": 60, "maxretries": 2, "accepted_statuscodes": ["302"], "max_redirects": 0, {{ service_alerting('zigbee2mqtt') }}}
  slzb-coordinator.json: |
    {"type": "port", "name": "SLZB-06M Coordinator", "hostname": "{{ slzb_ip }}", "port": 6638, "interval": 60, "maxretries": 2, "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
  homepage-k8s.json: |
    {"type": "http", "name": "k3s homepage", "url": "https://homepage.local.{{ domain }}", "interval": 60, "maxretries": 2, "accepted_statuscodes": ["302"], "max_redirects": 0, "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
  peanut-k8s.json: |
    {"type": "http", "name": "k3s peanut", "url": "https://peanut.local.{{ domain }}", "interval": 60, "maxretries": 2, "accepted_statuscodes": ["302"], "max_redirects": 0, "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
  # Successor to the autokuma-derived "NUT" monitor that retired with the Docker
  # container (D6 reversed, 2026-08-14). A TCP check of upsd over its ClusterIP Service
  # — the pod's own liveness probe (upsc) owns UPS-side depth; this owns "upsd
  # reachable", which is what the host's secondary upsmon depends on.
  nut-k8s.json: |
    {"type": "port", "name": "k3s nut (upsd)", "hostname": "nut.{{ k8s_namespace }}.svc.cluster.local", "port": 3493, "interval": 60, "maxretries": 2, "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
  grafana-k8s.json: |
    {"type": "http", "name": "k3s Grafana", "url": "https://grafana.local.{{ domain }}", "interval": 60, "maxretries": 2, "accepted_statuscodes": ["302"], "max_redirects": 0, "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
  # Tokenless probe of the mcp gate. It used to request /mcp with no bearer and accept the 404
  # that matches no router — but that body is Traefik's own `404 page not found`, identical to
  # what a router-less edge returns for every host, so a tile named "edge gate" was by
  # construction incapable of seeing the total-404 outage of #1322 and read 1 throughout it.
  # It now probes the app's own /health through a LAN-only Path router
  # (roles/k8s/traefik/templates/livesync-gate-secret.yaml.j2, homelab-mcp-probe), which
  # answers 200 `ok` only when the edge routes AND the pod answers. #1341.
  homelab-mcp-k8s.json: |
    {"type": "keyword", "name": "k3s homelab-mcp (edge gate)", "url": "https://mcp.local.{{ domain }}/health", "keyword": "ok", "interval": 60, "maxretries": 2, "max_redirects": 0, "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
  home-assistant-k8s.json: |
    {"type": "http", "name": "k3s Home Assistant", "url": "https://home-assistant.local.{{ domain }}", "interval": 60, "maxretries": 2, {{ service_alerting('home-assistant') }}}
  # Transcribed 2026-08-10 (Phase D.2 step 3): the tile probes the CLUSTER loki-homelab
  # through its
  # ClientIP-gated route (this pod's CIDR is admitted; hostAliases pin the -k8s name to
  # the VIP). /ready 503s ~15s after a restart while the ingester warms — brief PENDING
  # after a deploy is normal.
  # ClusterIP, not the edge route, since 2026-08-24 — see networkpolicy-loki-homelab.yaml.j2.
  # Probing through the route is what forced that route to admit the whole pod CIDR, and with
  # PathPrefix(`/`) on an auth_enabled:false Loki that handed every pod the full API. /ready
  # still 503s ~15s after a restart while the ingester warms, so brief PENDING after a deploy
  # remains normal.
  loki.json: |
    {"type": "http", "name": "Loki", "url": "http://loki-homelab.{{ k8s_namespace }}.svc.cluster.local:3100/ready", "interval": 60, "maxretries": 2, "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
  # Phase D scrutiny move (2026-08-10, SC5): /api/health rides the LAN Authelia bypass
  # (this pod's CIDR ⊂ 10.0.0.0/8), so a plain 200 — no 302 dance. Covers the cluster
  # web+influxdb pair; the per-node collectors are covered by SMART Data Freshness.
  scrutiny-k8s.json: |
    {"type": "http", "name": "k3s Scrutiny", "url": "https://scrutiny.local.{{ domain }}/api/health", "interval": 60, "maxretries": 2, "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
  karakeep-k8s.json: |
    {"type": "http", "name": "k3s karakeep", "url": "https://karakeep.local.{{ domain }}", "interval": 60, "maxretries": 2, "accepted_statuscodes": ["302"], "max_redirects": 0, "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
  # ClusterIP, not the edge route, since 2026-08-24. The route's ClientIP guard had been widened
  # to the whole pod CIDR so this prober could reach it, which meant every pod could read the
  # unauthenticated private calendars. Both callers moved in-cluster so a podSelector can gate
  # them (ical-proxy's `netpol_from` in host_vars/daniel-box.yml admits this pod and homepage). The tile therefore no
  # longer exercises the Traefik route — accepted, because nothing reaches ical-proxy through the
  # edge any more.
  ical-proxy-k8s.json: |
    {"type": "http", "name": "iCal Proxy k8s", "url": "http://ical-proxy.{{ k8s_namespace }}.svc.cluster.local:5000/calendar1.ics", "interval": 60, "maxretries": 2, "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
  code-server-k8s.json: |
    {"type": "http", "name": "k3s code-server", "url": "https://code-server.local.{{ domain }}", "interval": 60, "maxretries": 2, "accepted_statuscodes": ["302"], "max_redirects": 0, "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
  pihole-k8s-dns.json: |
    {"type": "dns", "name": "Pi-hole k8s DNS", "hostname": "homepage.local.{{ domain }}", "dns_resolve_server": "{{ dns_k8s_vip }}", "dns_resolve_type": "A", "port": 53, "interval": 60, "maxretries": 2, {{ service_alerting('pihole') }}}
  # ── off-cluster services ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # Email-to-RSS is a Cloudflare Worker, not a homelab workload (docs/email-to-rss.md), so
  # nothing else in the alerting spine sees it — this tile is its only coverage. Probing
  # /admin/login for a 200 checks that the route is live AND the script runs: the login page
  # is the only thing that emits a 200 there. The obvious alternative, a 404 from
  # /rss/<bogus>, is worse — the catch-all `app.all('*')` returns the same 404, so a deploy
  # that broke the router would keep the tile green. Interval 300 (not the LAN tiles' 60):
  # this leaves the house, and Cloudflare's own availability isn't worth a 60s poll.
  # Ingest (ForwardEmail → /api/inbound → KV) stays unmonitored — see the doc.
  # No accepted_statuscodes/max_redirects pair here, unlike the -k8s tiles above: those
  # exist to stop a 302 being followed into an Authelia 200. This endpoint answers 200
  # directly (verified), so the server-side ["200-299"] default is exactly right.
  email-to-rss.json: |
    {"type": "http", "name": "Email-to-RSS (Worker)", "url": "https://email-rss.{{ domain }}/admin/login", "interval": 300, "maxretries": 2, "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
  # ── push monitors fed by host crons (tokens unchanged — monitors keep their history) ───
  # docker-fleet + autofix-disk-prune keys removed at the Docker uninstall (2026-08-14):
  # their producers (the fleet-health cron, the disk-prune cron) retired with the
  # daemon. AutoKuma ON_DELETE=delete removes the live monitors on apply.
  crowdsec-home-allowlist.json: |
    {"type": "push", "name": "CrowdSec Home Allowlist", "description": "A daniel-box cron runs every 5 min: it looks up the home public IPv4 and IPv6 prefix via ipify and keeps them in the cluster LAPI home-ips allowlist via cscli. DOWN means an ipify lookup or cscli allowlists add failed; the message says which. Check syslog tag crowdsec-home-allowlist and the crowdsec pod.", "interval": 600, "max_retries": 0, "resendInterval": {{ uptime_kuma_k8s_push_resend_down_beats }}, "push_token": "{{ monitor_bridge_home_allowlist_push_token }}", "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
  crowdsec-remote-allowlist.json: |
    {"type": "push", "name": "CrowdSec Remote Allowlist", "description": "A daniel-box cron runs every 5 min: it reads Traefik's access log for 2xx responses on Authelia-protected routers and keeps each public client address in the cluster LAPI remote-ips allowlist for 7 days via cscli. DOWN means kubectl or cscli failed, no router carries the authelia middleware, or the list is at its cap; the message says which. Check syslog tag crowdsec-remote-allowlist and cscli allowlists inspect remote-ips.", "interval": 600, "max_retries": 0, "resendInterval": {{ uptime_kuma_k8s_push_resend_down_beats }}, "push_token": "{{ crowdsec_remote_allowlist_push_token }}", "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
  # DECIDED: 360s, not 300 — the deadline must exceed the producer's PERIOD, and the period
  # is the cron plus the work. Both DDNS pods run the image's default UPDATE_CRON=@every 5m
  # and ping Kuma at the END of each cycle, after IP detection and the record check, so a
  # beat lands at 300s + that cycle's work. The image caps the work at 5s (IP detection) +
  # 30s (record updating), so the pathological beat is 335s and 360 leaves 25s of margin —
  # not the 60s the round number suggests. Re-derive from those two timeouts, not from the
  # observed few seconds, if the image's defaults ever move. At interval 300 it raced every cycle
  # and lost ~7 times a day: 47 + 40 flap episodes over the 7 days to 2026-08-23, each a
  # ~2-minute dip with nothing actually wrong. Fixed here rather than by setting
  # UPDATE_CRON=@every 4m in both deployments — that edits two files and makes the
  # Cloudflare API busier to cure a monitoring artifact. Raise this if the cadence changes.
  cloudflare-ddns-proxied-push.json: |
    {"type": "push", "name": "Cloudflare DDNS Proxied", "description": "The cloudflare-ddns-proxied Deployment (favonia/cloudflare-ddns) beats only after a successful update of the proxied apex A record. DOWN means no successful update in 6 min: the pod is not running or Cloudflare API calls are failing. Read the cloudflare-ddns-proxied pod logs first.", "interval": 360, "max_retries": 0, "resendInterval": {{ uptime_kuma_k8s_push_resend_down_beats }}, "push_token": "{{ cloudflare_ddns_proxied_push_token }}", "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
  cloudflare-ddns-direct-push.json: |
    {"type": "push", "name": "Cloudflare DDNS Direct", "description": "The cloudflare-ddns-direct Deployment (favonia/cloudflare-ddns) beats only after a successful update of the unproxied terraria and valheim A records. DOWN means no successful update in 6 min: the pod is not running or Cloudflare API calls are failing. Read the cloudflare-ddns-direct pod logs first.", "interval": 360, "max_retries": 0, "resendInterval": {{ uptime_kuma_k8s_push_resend_down_beats }}, "push_token": "{{ cloudflare_ddns_direct_push_token }}", "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
  longhorn-backup.json: |
    {"type": "push", "name": "k3s Longhorn Backup", "description": "A daniel-box cron runs longhorn_backup_health.py every 10 min with the read-only kubeconfig: backup target availability, per-tier freshness, errored backups, failed jobs, the restore drill stamp and restore coverage. DOWN names the failing check. Run journalctl -t longhorn-backup-health, then check the Longhorn UI.", "interval": 1200, "max_retries": 0, "resendInterval": {{ uptime_kuma_k8s_push_resend_down_beats }}, "push_token": "{{ longhorn_backup_push_token }}", "tag_names": [{"name": "tag-severity", "value": "critical"}, {"name": "tag-runbook", "value": "https://docs.local.{{ domain }}/longhorn-disaster-recovery/"}], "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}", "email"]}
  daniel-box-disk.json: |
    {"type": "push", "name": "daniel-box Disk", "description": "A daniel-box cron runs df on / every 10 min, independent of Prometheus. DOWN means the root LV is over 75 percent used, the point where Longhorn stops scheduling replicas, or df returned nothing. Run df -h / on daniel-box; Longhorn replicas, container images and etcd all live on that volume.", "interval": 1200, "max_retries": 0, "resendInterval": {{ uptime_kuma_k8s_push_resend_down_beats }}, "push_token": "{{ daniel_box_disk_push_token }}", "tag_names": [{"name": "tag-severity", "value": "critical"}], "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}", "email"]}
{# The weekly registry garbage collection (k8s/registry role, registry-gc.sh). INTERVAL IS DERIVED FROM THE
   CRONTAB: `20 4 * * 0` (registry_k8s_gc_cron_* in the registry defaults), a 604800s period,
   plus one hour — the same grace the dead-man carries (docs/healthchecks-io-deadman.md) and
   comfortably over registry_k8s_gc_deadline_s (1200s) plus the scale-up wait. Gated on the
   token like the tiles below: an ungated tile sits red from creation until the secret exists. #}
{% if registry_gc_push_token | default('') %}
  registry-gc.json: |
    {"type": "push", "name": "Registry GC", "description": "A weekly daniel-box cron (Sunday 04:20) scales the registry to zero, runs a garbage-collect Job and scales it back. DOWN means GC was skipped for in-flight builds, the pod did not stop, the Job timed out, or the registry did not come back; the message says which. Check the registry-gc Job logs and the Deployment.", "interval": 608400, "max_retries": 0, "resendInterval": {{ uptime_kuma_k8s_push_resend_down_beats }}, "push_token": "{{ registry_gc_push_token }}", "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
{% endif %}
{# The host half of the shutdown chain. Its producer is nut_host's root cron, whose cadence and
   this deadline both derive from nut_host_watchdog_interval_minutes, so a schedule change cannot
   leave the tile graced against the old one. Gated on the token like its siblings: an ungated
   tile sits red from creation until the secret exists. Carries `email` because a dead secondary
   is only discovered at a power cut, which is exactly when Discord is unreachable.

   ONE TILE PER HOST, never shared: nut_host's watchdog runs on both daniel-box and
   daniel-server (ups_host), each pushing its OWN token (kuma-push.env.j2 selects by
   inventory_hostname). Two hosts on one push token would let either host's `up` satisfy the
   deadline, masking the other's `down`. #}
{% if ups_secondary_push_token | default('') %}
  ups-secondary.json: |
    {"type": "push", "name": "UPS Secondary (daniel-box)", "description": "The ups-secondary-health.sh cron on daniel-box runs every 10 min and reads upsmon.conf, systemctl is-active nut-monitor and upsc ups.status. DOWN means the secondary upsmon there is not configured, not running, or cannot reach upsd, so daniel-box would not power off on FSD. Run those two commands on daniel-box.", "interval": {{ nut_host_watchdog_interval_minutes | default(10) * 60 * 2 }}, "max_retries": 0, "resendInterval": {{ uptime_kuma_k8s_push_resend_down_beats }}, "push_token": "{{ ups_secondary_push_token }}", "tag_names": [{"name": "tag-severity", "value": "critical"}], "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}", "email"]}
{% endif %}
{% if ups_secondary_daniel_server_push_token | default('') %}
  ups-secondary-daniel-server.json: |
    {"type": "push", "name": "UPS Secondary (daniel-server)", "description": "The ups-secondary-health.sh cron on daniel-server runs every 10 min and reads upsmon.conf, systemctl is-active nut-monitor and upsc ups.status. DOWN means its secondary upsmon is not configured, not running, or cannot reach upsd, so daniel-server would not power off on FSD. Run those two commands on daniel-server.", "interval": {{ nut_host_watchdog_interval_minutes | default(10) * 60 * 2 }}, "max_retries": 0, "resendInterval": {{ uptime_kuma_k8s_push_resend_down_beats }}, "push_token": "{{ ups_secondary_daniel_server_push_token }}", "tag_names": [{"name": "tag-severity", "value": "critical"}], "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}", "email"]}
{% endif %}
{# The Loki read route's only witness. Its caller is a host process, and the route's
   guard is a ClientIP set of node-owned addresses, so no pod can probe it — see
   roles/setup/initial_setup/templates/loki-read-route-health.sh.j2.

   ONE TILE PER NODE, for the same reason as the UPS pair above: each host arrives at the route as
   a different source address, so the route can break on one node and not the other, and
   a shared token would let daniel-box's `up` cover a dead route from daniel-server.

   7200s: the deadline must exceed the PRODUCER'S period, and the producer is an hourly cron.
   3600 plus the run leaves 7200 with an hour of margin — move this and the cron minute together
   or neither. No `email`: a dead read route costs an operator a confusing 404 during triage, not
   a service outage, so Discord is proportionate. #}
{% if loki_route_witness_push_token | default('') %}
  loki-read-route-daniel-box.json: |
    {"type": "push", "name": "Loki Read Route (daniel-box)", "description": "The hourly kuma-check-loki-read-route timer on daniel-box runs probe.py loki-labels through the loki-homelab IngressRoute and judges the body, not the exit code; it reruns every 15 min while red. DOWN means the route answered 404 or an error from this host, usually the ClientIP guard rejecting its source address. Run systemctl status kuma-check-loki-read-route on daniel-box; check traefik pod placement.", "interval": 7200, "max_retries": 0, "resendInterval": {{ uptime_kuma_k8s_push_resend_down_beats }}, "push_token": "{{ loki_route_witness_push_token }}", "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
{% endif %}
{% if loki_route_witness_daniel_server_push_token | default('') %}
  loki-read-route-daniel-server.json: |
    {"type": "push", "name": "Loki Read Route (daniel-server)", "description": "The hourly kuma-check-loki-read-route timer on daniel-server runs probe.py loki-labels through the loki-homelab IngressRoute and judges the body, not the exit code; it reruns every 15 min while red. DOWN means the route answered 404 or an error from this host, usually the ClientIP guard rejecting its source address. Run systemctl status kuma-check-loki-read-route on daniel-server; check traefik pod placement.", "interval": 7200, "max_retries": 0, "resendInterval": {{ uptime_kuma_k8s_push_resend_down_beats }}, "push_token": "{{ loki_route_witness_daniel_server_push_token }}", "notification_name_list": ["{{ kuma_notification_id }}"]}
{% endif %}
  # DECIDED: this id stays `claude-otel-health` after the k8s/claude-otel role was renamed
